+\begin{proof}
+We first establish extension of indistinguishable pins.  Give an instance one
+additional label and regard its pinned value as a function of the value at that
+label.  Among the finitely many values in the disjoint union of the two domains,
+there are only finitely many distinct evaluation profiles.  Choose finitely many
+instances separating these profiles.  Over the coefficient field, products of
+normalized linear factors give an indicator polynomial for any one profile.
+Labelled gluing represents products of pinned values; the empty instance
+represents the constant one.  Thus each indicator polynomial is a linear
+combination of pinned values of finite instances.  Closing the additional label
+sums this indicator over its domain.  If a profile realized in the first domain
+were absent from the second, these two sums would be a positive integer and
+zero, respectively.  Characteristic zero and the assumed equality of all
+instances exclude this possibility.  Each prescribed additional pin therefore
+has an indistinguishable matching pin in the other domain.
+
+Apply this extension repeatedly to enumerate the first domain, and then apply
+it in the reverse direction to enumerate the second.  The resulting paired
+pins cover both domains and still agree on every instance.  In particular,
+single-constraint instances give agreement of their atomic table diagrams.
+This induces a bijection between twin classes: values at paired labels have
+matching one-coordinate atomic profiles, and the labels cover every possible
+choice of the other coordinates.  Indicator polynomials for these finite atomic
+profiles, followed by closing one additional label, show that corresponding
+twin classes have equal sizes.  Choose a bijection within each corresponding
+pair of classes.  The atomic diagram and invariance under twin replacement show
+that the union of these bijections is a domain isomorphism.  Each original pin
+is sent to a twin of its prescribed counterpart.  The converse is the change
+of summation variables and twin invariance described below.
+
+The construction uses unrestricted labelled gluing, including repeated
+constraints and scope positions.  It does not assert the simple-instance
+implication.  The pin-extension, atomic-diagram, and twin-multiplicity steps are
+also proved in the accompanying Lean formalization.
+\end{proof}
 
